   205	It was a small thing to see, and he did not think the benches saw it. The four district evaluators had been writing in a row, and at that line they stopped, one after another, like men on a road who see the one ahead halt. They were working officers. Every one of them had stood practitioners in frames for years, under a protocol that carried the Archmarshal's certificate as a matter of course, as a loaf carries a baker's mark; that office had been the protocol's evaluator of record since before any of them was born. It had been asked twice for its name and had not given it, and the form had no line for why.
   206	
   207	The evaluator nearest the records table looked at Ilsev. Ilsev did not look at him. She was looking at the clerk, with her file shut on her knee, and her face told Cael nothing whatever.
   208	
   209	On the fourth bench Gault had not moved. He was the house's own evaluator of record, the only man in that room who was called so every day, and he had declined nothing in his life. He sat with his case straight on his knees and his eyes on the pasted form, and then he took out his small book and wrote a line in it.
   210	
   211	"So entered," said the hood, and the pens began again.
   212	
   213	That was the whole of it. Five words had gone out from a desk by a noon dispatch, after a man had weighed them for four days on a post road and found them worth sending though they would stop nothing. They had stopped nothing. They were in the minute as an absence, between the count of the exhibits and the count of the orders, and the clerk was already reading the next line.
   214	
   215	Cael found that it was enough, and it surprised him. He had wanted a wall, a month ago, and had been given a blank on a form. But he had watched Gault hand up a leaf because it was there, and he had heard Karis spend fifty-one minutes on a room that could not be moved, and he thought he was beginning to learn how the honest ones did it. They did not do it to win. They did the thing that was theirs to do, to its full weight, and let it be entered.
   216	
   217	"The seat reserves," said the hood. "It will give its ruling upon the last Fifth-day of the academic year, at the hour after noon. From the First-day of that week the parties will keep within the bell, at the seat's call and the evaluation seat's. So ordered."
   218	
   219	"So ordered."
   220	
   221	The seat rose, and went out by its own door.
