    49	There was a wool press in the yard behind the guesting-house, and by the second morning Brom and Lira had found a use for it.
    50	
    51	Cael came out onto the gallery with the satchel on his shoulder and looked down. The yard was flagged, with a pump in one corner and a stack of empty woolsacks under a pentice roof. In the middle stood the press, a thing as old as the building: two oak posts as thick as a man, a beam across their tops, and a great wooden screw coming down through the beam to a platen of black timber six feet square. A capstan with four long bars turned the screw. In sale weeks, the woman of the house had said, it took four men to bale a sack with it. It was not a sale week.
    52	
    53	Brom stood under the platen.
    54	
    55	He had his coat off and his feet set and his forearms crossed above his head, the right over the left, and the platen rested on them. Lira was at the capstan with both hands on a bar and her heels dug in, walking it round a quarter turn at a time. With every quarter the black timber came down a finger's breadth, and Brom took it.
    56	
    57	"More," said Brom.
    58	
    59	"That's all of me. I weigh what I weigh."
    60	
    61	"It's not enough. Ephram put more on than that in the old frames, and the clerk said they'd set it a little over." He did not sound out of breath, only patient. "It's steady, as well. A frame isn't steady. It comes down on its springs all at once."
    62	
    63	Lira let go of the bar and stood back with her hands on her hips and looked at the press as she looked at a floor.
    64	
    65	"All at once," she said.
    66	
    67	She walked away from it, ten paces across the flags, and turned.
    68	
    69	Cael saw what she was going to do a beat before she did it. He had no time to say anything, and Brom, under the platen, could not see her.
    70	
    71	She ran the count, quite small, on wet flags, front foot and back foot and shoulders. Then she went. It was one low burst, flat across the yard, and the end of it was the capstan bar, a round of ash no thicker than her wrist and three feet off the ground. She came down on it with her right foot.
    72	
    73	The bar dropped under her and the screw turned, and the platen came down on Brom's crossed forearms like the lid of a chest.
    74	
    75	His knees gave an inch, and held.
    76	
    77	Cael had let the old read go out across the flags without deciding to. It came back to him as it always did, in weight and where the weight went. The platen's whole mass, and a girl's, and the speed she had brought with her, ran down through two forearms into the shoulders, both alike now, and down the long back and through the heels into the stone. The skin had gone dull at the wrists. Brom stood under a wool press like a third post.
    78	
    79	Lira was standing on the capstan bar on one foot with her arms out, perfectly balanced, grinning.
    80	
    81	"*That's* a little over," she said.
    82	
    83	"Again," said Brom.
    84	
    85	They did it nine times. She had four free bursts on stone in a day, and she spent them, and after that she ran at the bar and jumped, which was not the same and was near enough. She took it on the right foot and then on the left, and once, for nobody's benefit that Cael could see, on the right with the left already swinging through for the step off. The bar was round and moving and wet, and she landed on it every time as she would have landed on a chalk mark. He thought of a girl in the east hall in the recess, touching one foot down and stepping to the other. He did not think she remembered it herself.
    86	
    87	On the ninth Brom said, "Enough," and she walked the capstan back, and the platen rose off him, and the skin went back to being skin.
    88	
    89	He sat down on the pump trough with his forearms on his knees. There were two red bars across them.
    90	
    91	"Three days," said Lira. She had hitched herself up onto a capstan bar and was swinging her feet. "And then three people in a draughty hall write *Iron* on you, and you're a rank nobody can take off."
    92	
    93	"They'll find it."
    94	
    95	"You say that as if it were bad weather."
    96	
    97	"I say it like I say anything." Brom turned his forearms over and looked at the marks. "I'll stand in it, and I'll hold what they set, and the plate will say what's so. I want that. I've wanted it since before I had a Path." He was quiet a moment. "It's the paper after that I've stopped being able to see properly. Somebody hands you a sheet. It says *found*, and there's a line at the bottom for your name."
    98	
    99	Lira stopped swinging her feet.
   100	
   101	"I went and looked at the district hall yesterday," she said. "From outside. It's three streets over. It's got a blue door, and a board beside it with the sittings on, and the first one after the year's close has six names. Mine's the fourth. Somebody's written *Wind* after it, and then *Halcenvane*." She picked at the tape on her wrist. "I stood there a good while. A carter asked me if I was lost."
   102	
   103	"Were you?"
   104	
   105	"Ask me after the Fifth-day."
   106	
   107	Neither of them said anything more. Brom pumped water over his forearms, and Lira jumped down and went to look at the screw's thread, and after a while she asked him how you would grease a thing like that, and he told her.
   108	
   109	On the gallery Cael stood with his hand on the satchel's new strap.
   110	
   111	They had each finished something, and he could see it from up here as he could see a floor from a tier. Brom's left shoulder took its whole share. Lira landed where she chose. Whatever a panel or a blue door might add to either of them was a line on a sheet. They had done the work in a harness room and a painted hall and a wool yard, with nobody writing.
   112	
   113	And there were two boxes roped under a bed, and a carter's book in a coat.
   114	
   115	He had told himself all year that whatever this cost him, it should not cost them. Karis had read that over his shoulder and said it was not how any of them were built. Looking down at the two of them at the pump, he understood for the first time what she had meant by it. It was not his to decide what they paid. It never had been. He could only see to it that when the day came, each of them was asked, and not carried.
