   194	
   195	They went away toward the town, the two of them, the girl holding the slip and reading it over as she walked. They passed within six feet of him. She had a scatter of freckles and one front tooth a little crooked, and she did not see him at all.
   196	
   197	He stood at the trough until they were out of sight.
   198	
   199	He had been arguing about himself for a year. He saw that now. It had been his enrollment and his file, the nine days of his renewal, his frame. Every leaf of a hundred and eighty had his name at its head. And the whole while, on every north road in the Compact, on every morning the stations opened, this had been going on behind a door that a decent man held for you.
   200	
   201	The seat would rule in two days. He had thought of the ruling, all year, as the thing that would decide. It would decide what the registry did. He understood, with the sun on the back of his neck, that it would leave him with a decision of his own all the same, and that it was not the one he had been preparing for. It had nothing to do with whether he could win. It was whether he would stand in a frame, knowing what was appended to it, and let it enter him as advised.
