     1	# Chapter 54 — Good Room
     2	
     3	"Exchange," said Rooke, and his hand came down for the third.
     4	
     5	Ephram did not walk this time. He came across the oak like a man who has been shown at last what he is fighting, and means to have as much of it as there is, with the sun behind him now and the lath a dark bar against it.
     6	
     7	Cael did not go to meet him. He stood on the north chalk, with his hip aching from the crest to the knee and seven bursts gone out of a day that gave six, and he went down the rule as he had gone down it on his knees in a chalk road.
     8	
     9	*The conditions are not to be made.* He had not made them. A man had written four words on the back of a chart and kept them until a seat was done.
    10	
    11	*Only inside a fight the other begins, in earnest, awake, and on purpose.* Ephram had begun it in a reading room at midnight, with five people listening, and he had grounded his lath in front of twelve hundred rather than be given less than all of it. Nobody had ever been more in earnest who did not also wish him harm.
    12	
    13	*The whole cost on paper first.* It was on a leaf in Hesk's book, in the satchel on the east tier at Brom's feet, with capitals across the foot of it.
    14	
    15	He did it with his eyes open.
    16	
    17	The first cut came, heel and hip and edge, high, from the sun. He did not slip it. He put up his left forearm, as a man puts up a guard, and opened the give, and let the edge land where he could read it.
    18	
    19	"Guard," said Rooke.
    20	
    21	It went through the give as it had gone through before, across the two bruises that were there, and for an instant the yard went white at its edges. He had been ready this time, and he held himself open in front of it, all of him, as he had held still in a cage. It was a line; that was the first thing. A Blade did not strike with a piece of ash. He said where an edge was, a hand's breadth out beyond the wood, and it was there because he had said so, and it was as thin as a thought.
    22	
    23	"You're letting me," said Ephram, between his teeth. He had checked, and stood a pace off with the lath raised and the heel not yet down, breathing hard, and he looked angrier than he had looked all afternoon. "I said I wanted all of it."
    24	
    25	"I'm not letting you." It was the truth, and it was as much of the truth as there was to give him. "I'm looking at it. I've watched that edge from a tier for three years and I've never once been under it. Let me look."
    26	
    27	Ephram stared at him. Then something went out of his shoulders, and what came into his face instead was not softer, but it was pleased.
    28	
    29	"Then look," he said, and put the heel down.
    30	
    31	He cut in earnest. Cael saw the whole engine of it, close, in the moment before it reached him: the heel that committed the body, and the hip that carried it, and the edge that could not then be called back by the man who had sent it. And he saw the other thing, which he had never understood from the rim. The line did not stop at the edge. It ran on, out past the lath's tip, straight as a rule, and Ephram *knew* along it; whatever was going to cross that line, he had word of it a breath before it came. That was how a lath had been lying at the end of a lane.
    32	
    33	Cael took it on the forearm, and kept everything he had turned toward it.
    34	
    35	"Guard," said Rooke, a long way off.
    36	
    37	They came together.
    38	
    39	It was quiet. The storm had come down on him like a roof, and the road had gone in like a key into a lock that had been cut for it, and this was smaller than either. It was like watching Hesk lay a steel rule along a line he had already scribed and find that it lay true.
    40	
    41	And for the length of one breath he could see edges.
    42	
    43	He could see every edge in the Crown yard. The coping of the rim wall stood out all round him like a drawn wire, and the joints of the oak, sixty feet of them, each a line with two sides. The risers of the tiers went up the sky in ruled steps, and the black boards had their corners. In front of him was the lath, with its true edge standing clear of the wood, and from its tip a thin bright line ran out through the air and ended on the third button of his own shirt. He knew where that line would be crossed, and by what, and it was the plainest thing he had ever known.
    44	
    45	He did not pick it up.
    46	
    47	It was there, new and whole, like a tool laid on the wrong side of the bench, and he left it lying, because it was not for this floor. A man had asked to be shown what he was fighting, and he was owed the fight with what Cael had carried down the steps, and with nothing that had been his own a breath ago. Cael shut it like a drawer, and the edges went out of the yard one by one, the coping last.
    48	
    49	He had only the old read then, and it was enough, because he had stopped waiting.
    50	
    51	Ephram's heel went down for another cut out of the last, and it was the short one, with no hip in it. Cael had it a half beat early, as he had had it in the first exchange, and this time he went.
    52	
    53	Eight. It was low, and inside, under the lath, along the line of Ephram's own arm, with the hip shrieking and the edge passing over his back. He came up off the landing beat with his right hand out, and laid two fingers on the inside of Ephram's wrist, on the tape, and gave it the spark.
    54	
    55	It went out of him like a word, and the flint smell came up between them. Ephram yelped, and the lath jumped in his hand, and then he was laughing again, helplessly, shaking his wrist.
    56	
    57	"Touch," said Rooke. "The enrollee."
    58	
    59	One each.
    60	
    61	---
    62	
    63	The tiers were on their feet for the fourth.
    64	
    65	He did not know when they had stood. He heard them now as he walked back to his chalk, a noise like the weir below Ostrand bridge, and under it somewhere the Stone's enormous voice saying one word over and over. He did not look up. His left forearm had four lines across it, and he could not have closed the hand to save his life; his shoulder had counted one push, and his hip was done with him.
    66	
    67	Ephram stood at the far chalk, soaked and grinning, with a red mark on his wrist.
    68	
    69	He had learned. Cael could see it in how the man set his feet. He had learned in three exchanges what a rule of the house had kept from him for three years: that a lane was a line, and could be read; that the push took a heel, if the heel went down slowly; and that the boy in front of him was not quick, but early, so that no chain of cuts would close on a man who knew each link before it was forged.
    70	
    71	So he would not chain. Cael read it in his weight before Rooke's hand had moved. Ephram was going to do the simplest thing a Blade could do: he would wait for a moment when there was only one place Cael could be, and then put everything he had into a single cut at that place, shoulders and all, the plain entry from the first year.
    72	
    73	"Exchange."
    74	
    75	He walked. It was the old walk, at every angle, with the lath low, but he was not walking to learn weight now. He was laying lines. He turned his wrist and there was one across the middle of the oak, level, a bar across a doorway, and he turned it again and there was a second, slantwise, from the rim wall to the first. He could not hold more than two at once; Cael saw that, and saw that he did not need to. He was building a corner out of two lines and four feet of sunk stone wall.
    76	
    77	Cael went back before it. Nine. A burst along the wall, and the hip gave him nothing in return but pain. Ephram turned with him, unhurried, and the lines turned too, and he spent a tenth to stay ahead of them. He had never gone past nine on any floor.
    78	
    79	He put the push into the oak under the walking heel, and Ephram stepped over the place as a man steps over a puddle. It took the shoulder four to learn that he had seen it coming. He tried it a fifth time, at the load, at the edge of his reach, and the boards jumped a foot to the left of a boot that was no longer there.
    80	
    81	Then the wall was at his back, with a flight of steps on his right hand, and a line lay on each side of him. Ephram stood ten feet off, and his heel was up. There was one place left, and both of them knew where it was.
    82	
    83	Cael asked the air to lean.
    84	
    85	He laid it out of the corner, down the only gap, fifteen feet along the foot of the wall. The dust woke in the joints and ran, and his ears closed and opened. It was a line a blind man could have read, and Ephram read it.
    86	
    87	His heel went down. It went down hard, for the end of the lane, with the hip coming after it and the shoulders dropped and all of him behind the edge. It was the plain entry, the whole weight at once; he had seen a lane once, and he knew to the foot where it would put a man down.
    88	
    89	He could not take it back.
    90	
    91	Cael did not step into the lane.
    92	
    93	He had a breath, perhaps, before the bill. He took the read back off the air and went the other way, a single stride, straight at the man, inside the arc of the cut, to the place where the lath's root would pass at the top of its swing, a foot from Ephram's own shoulder. He set his feet there. He put up his left forearm as a post, and he shut the give.
    94	
    95	He sent the load to the floor. It was Brom's gift, the first thing he had ever taken from Brom, and he had watched it practised in a harness room for a year: nothing in the whole of him pushing back, only somewhere for the weight to go, down the forearm and the shoulder, down the back, through both heels into sprung oak.
    96	
    97	The cut arrived.
    98	
    99	It had been meant for empty air fifteen feet away. It met a thing that did not give, a hand's breadth from where it began, with a Blade's whole committed body still coming on behind it. Cael felt the edge go into his forearm to the bone, it seemed, and stop. He felt the load go through him and into the boards, and the oak took it and held.
   100	
   101	It could not go on, and so it went back.
   102	
   103	He heard it. He had heard the same sound once in a chalk cut, under the noise of Iron Skin holding: a click, and a give. Ephram's right arm stopped being a thing with an edge on it, and the lath fell out of his hand and rang on the oak.
   104	
   105	Cael's right palm was flat on his chest. He had put it there in the same beat, as he had meant to.
   106	
   107	"Touch," said Rooke.
   108	
   109	Ephram went down on one knee.
   110	
   111	He did not cry out. He knelt on the pale boards with his right arm hanging from the shoulder at an angle no arm should hang at, and his face the colour of the chalk, and the Crown yard, which had been roaring, made a sound like a bellows let go.
   112	
   113	Then the bill came in, and everything Cael owned went slow.
   114	
   115	*One.* He tried to get down beside him and his knees bent a beat after he asked. *Two.* "Healer," Rooke was saying, in the same voice in which he had called the touch, and a spare grey woman was already off her bench and coming along the stand with her bag. *Three.* Somebody very large had come over the rim wall without troubling about the steps.
   116	
   117	"Don't move it," said Brom. He was on his knees on the oak with one hand flat between Ephram's shoulder blades. "I've had one out. It goes back. It goes back worse if you help."
   118	
   119	"That's the door," said Ephram.
   120	
   121	He said it through his teeth, with his eyes shut, and Cael, slow, kneeling at last, thought that he had not heard right.
   122	
   123	"Every Blade's got a door in him. They tell you in the second year. They don't tell you where." Ephram opened his eyes, which were wet with pain, and quite clear. "I've wondered where mine was for three years. Nobody on this hill was ever early enough to stand in it." He breathed. "Did you know it would do that?"
   124	
   125	"No," said Cael. *Six. Seven.* The word came a beat late, and it was true. He had known that a commitment could not be taken back, and he had not once asked himself where it went if it was met. "I didn't price it. I priced my arm."
   126	
   127	"Good," said Ephram. "I'd not have liked it if you'd known and done it anyway." He turned his head a little toward the stand. "Coach. Call it. I'm not having it set till it's called."
   128	
   129	Rooke looked down at the two of them from the edge of the stand with his glass in his hand. Whatever he felt, his face showed about as much as his slate did.
   130	
   131	"Two touches to one," he said, to twelve hundred people. "The enrollee."
   132	
   133	Then the healer had Ephram's wrist in one hand and his elbow in the other, and Brom had him round the chest. She said something short, and there was a second sound like the first, and Ephram made a noise that he afterward denied.
   134	
   135	*Ten.*
   136	
   137	At eleven Cael could have stood, and he stayed where he was.
   138	
   139	---
   140	
   141	It had been waiting for him since the third exchange.
   142	
   143	He had felt it there all through the fourth, in the place behind his eyes that belonged to nothing else, patient as a clerk. He looked at it now, on his knees on the oak, with the yard coming apart into noise above him and the healer winding linen round a captain's arm.
   144	
   145	```
   146	FRAGMENT ACQUIRED
   147	[unnamed] — Blade-adjacent. Duration: sustained. Integration: partial.
   148	Tier equivalent: Iron.
   149	Note: edge-declaration component; line-reading component.
   150	Contact range.
   151	Acquisition: directed. Engagement: adversarial, earnest — non-hostile.
   152	```
   153	
   154	He read it through twice.
   155	
   156	Nine.
   157	
   158	*Iron.* It was the lightest thing he had been handed since before Norhold, and Karis's curve had been right again, for it had come in a breath. *Edge-declaration.* That was the edge that stood clear of the wood because a man said so. *Line-reading.* It was the thin bright rule that had ended on his third button. *Contact range.* He had five lines across his forearm that said the same.
   159	
   160	And at the top, where a name went, *unnamed*, as it had said on the road. It always said so. He had sat on a chalk bank and grieved that he would never be able to find the woman it had come from.
   161	
   162	He knew this one's name, and where he slept, and what he whistled on a stair, and that he could not get the third line of a song right. Ephram was eighteen inches from him, having his arm bound, and he could have said it now. That was the difference, and it was the cost. He had written it down the night before at full weight, and it weighed exactly what he had written.
   163	
   164	He read the last line a third time.
   165	
   166	*Earnest — non-hostile.*
   167	
   168	He had not known the notices had those words in them. The one before had said *combat*. The storm had come out of a Gold who was trying to beat him, and the road out of a woman who meant to put him in a wagon. This had come out of a man who had asked for the bout because he wished to know the truth about a friend, and who had fought with everything he had and wished him no harm in the world. Whatever kept the notices had known the difference, and it had a word for it.
   169	
   170	"Coach."
   171	
   172	It was Rooke's clerk, the thin anxious young man who had kept the visitors' ledger. He had come to the edge of the stand with a book open on each forearm, and he looked from one to the other like a man holding two plates.
   173	
   174	"Which book, Coach? It isn't a program bout, so it oughtn't to go in the program's. And it isn't a ladder bout, so I don't see how it goes in the house's. There's no head for it in either."
   175	
   176	Rooke did not turn round. He was watching Brom and the healer get Ephram to his feet.
   177	
   178	"Both. Enter both."
   179	
   180	"And for him? There's no column that—"
   181	
   182	"As chalked."
   183	
   184	The clerk looked up at the first board, at the two words Rooke had written there in the dark, and then he sat down at the long table and dipped his pen. He wrote it twice, once in each book, as a man does who has been told to get a thing down where nobody can rub it off.
   185	
   186	Cael stood up, and his legs allowed it.
   187	
   188	At the far end of the stand, Gault had not moved from beside his case. The magister was looking across the oak at him, steadily, as he had looked across a trestle every week for two years with three cards and a glass. He had signed a figure for every one of those weeks, and every figure had been earned on the house's floors, to the decimal, exactly as it was written; and he had just been shown what they had been figures of.
   189	
   190	He did not take out his small book. For once he did not seem to need to write a thing down in order to have it.
   191	
   192	He nodded. It was one small exact nod, the kind he gave a card that had been added right, and Cael nodded back, and found that he had to look somewhere else quickly.
   193	
   194	The yard was a great deal of noise. He had expected that, and had not expected the kinds. The Current girl was standing on her bench with both fists in the air and tears running down her face, and seemed not to know about the tears. The assistant had her hands over her mouth. Half of Ephram's cohort were over the rail and round him, and the porter stood by his pail with his cap in his hand, blowing his nose and speaking severely to the ground. Brom walked beside the healer with the captain's good arm across his shoulders, and his eyes were wet, and he did not wipe them; he had said once that a man who looks at a hurt only teaches it to be looked at.
   195	
   196	Karis was not crying. She was on her feet, clapping, with her pale fingers flat, and her face was the one she wore when a count came out.
   197	
   198	Lira was not crying either. She had her arms folded and her chin up, and she was looking round the tiers at twelve hundred faces, one after another. She had sat on a painted floor and told him she knew what their faces would do, and she seemed to be checking it.
   199	
   200	At the top of the west tier the seat was empty. The grey man had gone, with his stick, to catch a bag. And at the end of the stand, by the porter's boards, there was nobody.
   201	
   202	---
   203	
   204	It took the Crown yard the better part of an hour to empty.
   205	
   206	He sat on the lowest step of the east flight while it did, because the healer had told him to sit somewhere and hold his arm above his heart. She had looked at the forearm and whistled through her teeth, and felt along both bones, and said that nothing was broken and that it would be the colour of a plum by morning and stay so for a fortnight. She had bound it, and then she had looked at the way he was sitting and asked which hip. He had told her, and she had said that she supposed there was a shoulder as well.
   207	
   208	"And behind the eyes," said Cael.
   209	
   210	"I don't strap those," said the healer, and went after her other patient.
   211	
   212	The town went first. It went up the tiers and out by the yard gate in a slow loud river, wool-factors and carters and clerks, and he heard his name in it, the registry's and the hill's, and a good many guesses, and let them go by. The lake house's Blade came down to the rim before he left and stood looking at the oak for some time with his lath across his shoulders; then he looked at Cael, and laughed, and shook his head, and went away.
   213	
   214	The house went more slowly, for it did not seem to know how to leave. First-years stood about in knots on the tiers, re-fighting the fourth exchange with their hands. Nobody came down the steps to him, and that was Lira's doing, who had put herself at the head of the east flight with her arms folded.
   215	
   216	Withrow was the last.
   217	
   218	She had not left the registrar's chair. She sat in it, upright, with her hands in her lap, while the stand emptied round her and Bracken went down with the clerk's two books under his arm, and she went on sitting until the tiers were bare stone again and the porter had begun at the top with his broom. Then she stood.
   219	
   220	She did not come to the steps. She looked at the oak, the whole length of it, as a woman looks at a room she has kept for a long time, and up at the first board, where four lines stood in a coach's capitals. Then she looked at Cael on his step, with his arm bound.
   221	
   222	Her face was not the registry face, and he did not know what it was. He thought that she had seen a thing on her own oak that explained two years of an enrollee looking ordinary, and that she was adding it, quite calmly, to the things she had decided not to ask about.
   223	
   224	She inclined her head to him. It was not the registry's depth; it was a good deal less, and it was meant.
   225	
   226	Then she went out by the north arch, alone, and she was the last out of the yard.
   227	
   228	On the tier above him the Current girl had come back for a scarf. She stopped beside Lira, red-eyed and hoarse.
   229	
   230	"I've trained on this hill with him for two years," she said. "I've watched every wing-three card there was. I never saw one bit of that. Not one." She wiped her nose on her wrist. "Where's he been *keeping* it?"
   231	
   232	Lira did not answer the question. She looked down the steps at him, and then out over the empty oak.
   233	
   234	"That's the first time this school has ever seen him," she said.
   235	
   236	---
   237	
   238	Ephram came back when the light had begun to go yellow.
   239	
   240	He came down the steps slowly, with his right arm bound across his chest in a sling of the healer's linen and his coat hung over the shoulder. He had washed. He carried the porter's chalk pail in his left hand, and he was carrying it badly.
   241	
   242	"There's a custom," he said. "You'll not know it. You've never been on a board." He set the pail down on the stand. "Whoever loses on the oak chalks it. With his own hand, the same day, where the other can see him do it. It's older than the brass on the Copper crown. The porter does the rest of the year, but he doesn't do that."
   243	
   244	"You've one arm."
   245	
   246	"I've the wrong one. Hold the ladder."
   247	
   248	Cael held the ladder, with his own one good hand. Ephram went up three rungs of it, left-handed, as carefully as a man crossing ice, and stopped level with the head of the third board, where the Iron column began.
   249	
   250	His own name stood there. *Ephram. Blade. Rank Six.* It had stood there, in one porter's chalk or another's, since before Cael came up the road.
   251	
   252	He took a lump of chalk out of his coat pocket and looked at the name for a moment. Then he reached up above it, to the bare black strip between the name and the board's painted heading, and drew a line. It was short and level, the width of the column, and it wavered, because it was drawn with the wrong hand.
   253	
   254	He wrote nothing above it.
   255	
   256	"I asked the porter this afternoon what he'd put," said Ephram, to the board. "He said there wasn't a column, and he'd want a fifth board, and the house wouldn't vote him the paint." He looked at his line. "So that's a rule, with the space over it left. Anybody who reads this board next year will see that the head of the Iron column has something above it, and that nobody could put a name to it. The first-years will ask. I'll tell them."
   257	
   258	He came down the ladder, one rung at a time, and stood on the stand with chalk on his fingers.
   259	
   260	"I said last night I wanted to find out," he said. "Well, I have. I had one touch off you, while you were still being careful, and I'll keep that one. I had none after. I've a shoulder that wants a month." He turned the chalk over in his left hand. "I was the best fighter on this hill for three years, by the only measure the hill has. And the measure was short the whole time, and I'm the man who kept the chart that proves it. Ninety-eight entries. I ruled the line myself."
   261	
   262	He looked up at the wavering rule above his name.
   263	
   264	"Still glad."
   265	
   266	He said it plainly, as he would have read out a rota.
   267	
   268	"I thought I would be. I wasn't sure. You don't know a thing like that until it's cost you something, and I'd rather it had cost me a shoulder than have gone on not knowing." He put the chalk in his pocket. "Go and be whatever that was, somewhere there's room for it. I'll be here. Somebody has to tell them it happened, and get the third line wrong."
   269	
   270	Cael stood with his hand still on the ladder.
   271	
   272	It was in him, new, housed for the moment where nothing else was, a thing with an edge and a rule to it. It had come out of the man in front of him an hour ago, and he could have said so in a dozen words. *There's something I took. You should know what you gave.*
   273	
   274	Ephram would have listened. He would have gone still, and thought about it, and very probably said that he was glad of that as well; he had turned his back on a table of papers twice, for a friend's sake, without being told what was on them.
   275	
   276	And then he would have known. He would have been a cohort captain at a chartered house who knew what the registry's unclassified enrollee was, and what he had carried east, and he would have known it on the day a district officer rode up to the gate, and for every day after. He was staying. He had a board with his name on it, and they would come and ask him, courteously, what he had seen.
   277	
   278	Cael did not say it.
   279	
   280	"You've a look on you," said Ephram.
   281	
   282	"I'm writing something down."
   283	
   284	"You always are." Ephram picked up his coat with the wrong hand and got it over his shoulder at the second try. "Don't make me sound clever in it. I walked into a post."
   285	
   286	He went up the steps, slowly, and at the top he began to whistle. It was the song from the residence windows, and the third line was wrong.
   287	
   288	---
   289	
   290	He wrote it at the rim, sitting on the coping with his feet over the oak. He wrote in pencil, as Karis had learned to, with the book on his knee and the bound arm steadying the page.
   291	
   292	*Nine. Taken in earnest from a friend. He asked for the fight and not for this, and gave it without knowing that he gave it. Iron weight. It came quietly, like a rule laid along a line already drawn. An edge that is where you say it is, and a line past the end of it that knows what will cross. I saw the whole yard by it for one breath. I put it down and did not pick it up again. The bout was fought with what I brought.*
   293	
   294	*He isn't told. That was priced last night and it's owing from today. It doesn't go yellow in a fortnight. He chalked a rule over his own name with his wrong hand and said he was still glad, and I stood there holding his ladder with a thing of his in me and said I was writing something down.*
   295	
   296	*Not priced: his shoulder. I knew a Blade can't take a commitment back. I never asked where it goes if somebody stands in the way. That's mine. He says he'd have minded more if I'd known. I mind that I didn't. Entered.*
   297	
   298	He read it over once, and then set down the bills, smaller.
   299	
   300	*Left forearm, wrist to elbow: the touch, and four more on the guard, the last to the bone or near it. Hip, crest to knee: ten on the oak, four past the six. Shoulder: six pushes at load, one past Norhold. Two lanes, ten and ten. Behind the eyes. One spark. Not the quiet thing; in a yard with twelve hundred people looking down it had nothing to offer and didn't. Not the one from the road.*
   301	
   302	He stopped at that, and looked at it, because it wanted its reason beside it, and the log had always been more honest than he was aloud.
   303	
   304	It had lifted its head a dozen times in four exchanges. A heel going down on oak was the plainest thing to fix in all the world, and Ephram had put his heel down thirty times; one point under that boot, set and told to stay, would have ended the bout in the first exchange. *There?* it had asked, each time, in its courteous way. *His*, he had told it. *Leave it.*
   305	
   306	*Why not. One: it's on no paper the registry holds, and a man with a pencil had the top of the west tier. Two: it came off somebody who used it to put people in a wagon, and I've not yet found a use for it that isn't a hand on a person. Three: he asked me for a fight, and it holds people. You don't do that to a friend who has asked you for a fight. It stays in the kit.*
   307	
   308	He shut the book and put it in the satchel, which Brom had left for him on the step with the strap done up.
   309	
   310	He had gone out by the yard gate after Ephram left, to put his head under the pump in the covered walk. It was a plain stone gate with a dressed lintel, and he had walked beneath it a thousand times, and halfway through, something had raised its hand.
   311	
   312	It was not the voice he knew. *There?* was a patient voice, and asked about resting things. This was quicker, and it did not ask about the lintel. It asked about the lintel's lower edge, the long straight arris of it over his head, where two faces of the stone met, and it held that edge up to him as a boy in a schoolroom holds up his slate.
   313	
   314	*Edge?*
   315	
   316	He had stood under the gate with a bound arm and very nearly laughed. It had taken him two days and forty proposals an hour to learn what to do with the last one, and he had a stair for this one to come up by.
   317	
   318	*Lintel*, he had told it. *It keeps the arch up. It isn't for cutting. Leave it.*
   319	
   320	It left it. He had felt it settle, in the place beside the other, as a second tool settles into a rack that somebody has already made room in. He supposed it would ask again. He supposed it would ask about every sill and step and table's edge between here and wherever they were going, for a day or two, and take names instead.
   321	
   322	He sat on at the rim.
   323	
   324	The sun had gone behind the lecture range. Below him the oak lay in its pit, pale and dry and empty, sixty feet by forty, with a few scuffs on it near the wall where a man had knelt. The porter had finished the tiers and gone, and up on the wall the four boards were going grey. He could still read the first of them, and on the third, at the head of a column, a short wavering line with nothing over it.
   325	
   326	He had come back from the pump by the covered walk, past the records hall.
   327	
   328	Its door had stood open, and the lamps were lit in pairs. Bracken was at his counter in his shirtsleeves, with all three pairs of glasses about him and a quire of printed forms at his elbow, on the registry's own stock, and Cael had been able to read their heading from the walk: *Withdrawal of an enrollee from a chartered house.* Bracken had counted four sheets off the quire and laid them in a row on the wood, under the lamp. He had squared each of them to the counter's edge with one finger, and then to its neighbour, and set the pen beside them.
   329	
   330	Four. He had not looked up. They were for morning.
   331	
   332	Seln had looked round a reading room at the top of a library once, on the evening of a birthday, with six people in it and a honey cake. *It is a good room*, he had said. *I have been in a great many.*
   333	
   334	Cael had been in a great many himself by now. He had been in a wool exchange with a lot number under his boot, and a dairy with a slit window, and a borrowed office with nothing of anybody's in it; he had stood on a north road outside a room he would never go into; and he had been careful in every one of them.
   335	
   336	He looked down at sixty feet of sprung oak in a hole in the ground, where for an hour that afternoon he had not been careful with anything.
   337	
   338	It was a yard, and had no roof. He said it anyway.
   339	
   340	"Good room."

