books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-7-He posted it openly. The board on the covered walk carried it in his flat hand: *Crown yard, third bell. Ephram — Cael. Sanctioned, earnest, rated.* No preamble on the board, no notation about the term's other business, nothing that a registry-literate person could read as an institution making a statement — just a bout, sanctioned, at a bell, the way the board had carried eight hundred bouts before it. Rooke did not make speeches and had found, across thirty years, that a coach's grief went best into a schedule.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-8-
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-9-The campus read it and understood it for what it was, and by the first bell of the next morning the town below the bluff had read it too. Bracken's counter took four separate inquiries about whether the yard's gallery was open to non-enrollees and answered all four in the same six words. The kitchen moved the afternoon meal an hour early without being asked. Two of the coaching staff rearranged their floors. And when Cael crossed the quadrangle at midday he counted the traffic on the covered walk against a normal Fifth-day and found it running at something near triple, all of it drifting past one board, most of it not stopping, because the bluff had manners and the bluff also had eyes: the Crown yard's last look at the enrollee the brackets could never hold, offered by the one fighter who had made himself wait for it longest.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-10-
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-11-He catalogued the walk on the way past, because the walk was going to stop being his in six days and he wanted the count accurate. Forty-one paces from the records hall's door to the yard gate. Nine boards, of which Rooke owned three. The notice in the flat hand, third board, second column, with the term's ordinary business above and below it, unremarkable, permanent-looking. He read it twice and went to eat something, and did not manage much of it.
