books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-23-The floor: the Crown yard's own, stone under the open sky, a rectangle of it laid in courses that ran east and west, sixty feet by forty, worn smooth in a broad oval at the middle where two centuries of fighting had polished it and rough at the corners where nobody ever went. Tiered seating on three sides, north, east and south, six rows of cut stone rising from the floor's edge; the west side open to the gate and the covered walk beyond it, which meant the wind came in at the west and the sun, at the third bell in the term's last month, came low over the south tier and laid the tiers' own shadow in bars across the floor's southern third. The boards' inlay at center: a compass figure of darker stone, four feet across, set flush, which Cael had learned to the handspan in his first month because a fighter who knows one fixed mark on a floor always knows where he is. The flags at east and west of it, twelve feet out. The rail along the west edge, where the cohort stood.
--
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-31-**First exchange.**
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-32-
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-33-Ephram came off the mark at the cohort's speed, which was not a speed Halcenvane's yard had built and not one Cael had ever been given at full — Blade doctrine at the rate Rooke had drilled into a whole cohort and then refined into one captain, edge-declarations chained faster than the tier's arithmetic ought to allow, each declaration's line laid a half-beat before his body arrived on it. Cael gave ground east, off the inlay, using the floor's open middle, and bought facts with the first thirty seconds the way the delegation bought facts with any first exchange.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-34-
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-35-The facts came fast and cost him for the buying. Ephram's entry line came off the left foot and always off the left foot; that was not a tell, that was doctrine, and Rooke's doctrine did not hide. The chained declarations ran in threes — three edges, a beat, three edges — and the beat between the threes was where the whole cohort reset and where the whole cohort was readable, and Ephram's beat was shorter than any of them, short enough that reading it did not buy time, only information. And the line-reading arrived where Cael's evasion was *going* rather than where it was, which was the thing the cohort's captain had that the cohort's fighters did not: Cael broke east and the third edge of the second three was already laid across the ground he had picked, and he had to spend a Wind burst to not be standing on it.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-36-
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-37-He paid for the burst inside four seconds, the way he always paid: the economy came off the top of the exchange's budget and stayed off. On the second three he took a wash off an edge-declaration across the left forearm — not a score, no flag, the edge's outer margin only — and the forearm went hot and stayed hot and was going to be a bruise the shape of a hand by morning.
