books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md:39:Flags: one to Ephram, at fifty-one seconds, clean, on the inlay's east lip, a contact-range score off the fourth chained three. The east tier made a sound and stopped itself. Ephram did not celebrate it, and did not look at the flag, and went back to his mark rolling the shoulder he had rolled at the taping, which Cael filed.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-40-
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-41-At the mark, Cael ran the honest total. He had bought: the threes, the short beat, the left-foot entry, and the fact that Ephram's line-reading was predictive and not reactive, which meant every evasion he made was a sentence he was completing for someone else. He had spent: one burst, a forearm, and the exchange. He had a decision to make and forty seconds of the break to make it in, and he made it at about the twelfth.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-42-
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-43-**Second exchange.**
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-44-
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-45-Ephram had watched him from the cohort's rail for a year. That was the whole strategic content of the second exchange, and Ephram opened it by proving it: he pressed the tempo past the band and refused the reset.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-46-
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-47-The band was the thing nobody at Halcenvane had ever named out loud and Ephram had charted from the rail across a year of Fifth-days — the rate at which the enrollee fought, which was a rate and not a ceiling, consistent to a degree that no honest fighter's output is ever consistent, a managed middle that never rose and never broke. It had a floor and it had a lid and Ephram had learned the distance between them well enough to fight inside it. So he did not fight inside it. He chained his edge-work without the beat, four threes with no reset in them, driving west and then north-west, and he drove Cael off the open middle and onto the north tier's foot, where the floor ran out at three feet of rough course-stone and the circling stopped.
--
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-51-Cael made the decision he had made at the mark, and the yard found out what it had been watching for two years.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-20.md-52-
