editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md-209-Neither of them said anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md-210-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md-211-It lasted perhaps as long as it takes to read a line. Bracken had written *or to her order* in his own hand, as he had lent an old enrollment book for a citation and not asked what for. He had read every leaf the house had filed all year, and counted the sittings, and seen the last Fifth-day coming since the year's first week. He was a registrar of thirteen years' standing, and there was nothing whatever in his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md-212-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md-213-"The third volume," he said. "You'll want it back by the second bell tomorrow. I ask when, not why."
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md-235-He thought about them for a while, the four men down there, senior, patient, changed on the bell. They had stood at that road since before the year opened, and they had been waiting all that time for one thing, a change in a column. A status. The riders had come, and the road, and after that nothing; and they had gone on standing there through a recess and eleven sittings with their tin cup, because the thing they were waiting for was being brought to them, a Fifth-day at a time, by the people they were watching.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md-236-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md-237-He had helped to bring it. He had sat up straight in a coach eleven times.
